WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:08.550
Filmmaker Don Swanson of Occurrence At Mills
Creek Movie: Hi!

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:13.140
My name is Don Swanson and I’m the writer
and director of Occurrence At Mills Creek

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.140
Movie.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:19.220
We are currently running an Indiegogo campaign
to raise funds to make a feature film based

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:21.330
on a short film.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:26.489
Which certainly isn’t anything new but our
approach is kind of unique to it that our

00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:31.989
short film is in actuality the opening act
of the feature film.

00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:37.190
All of this started back in 2016 when I was
working on another project with a young actress

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.440
named Alexa Mechling.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.320
And she was being kind of hyper or whatever
and so I joked with her and said that if she

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.490
didn’t settle down I was going to write
a script where her throat.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:50.930
And about a year and a half later she asked
me what was going on with that idea and basically

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:56.450
at the time we had come off of that feature,
had done another project and quite honestly

00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:03.200
I was pretty tired and was just looking to
do something kind of fun on a weekend where

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.480
we could do some cool, cool horror stuff.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:14.460
And the idea that I came up with was what
is the worst fate that a daughter could suffer.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:23.570
And in my mind that would be emotionally and
psychically and mentally tormented and betrayed

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:29.330
by her mother and so that’s where the throat
came in and everything.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:35.920
The idea that a mom was messing with her daughter,
kind of punishing her mentally and emotionally

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.100
before taking the price of her life.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:46.549
And I kind of worked on the script and got
it to a pretty good point and some people

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:47.870
became aware of it.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:53.840
And that’s how we landed Betsy Lynn George
ultimately and it was actually Betsy who suggest

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.790
maybe it was the sister because she saw the
mom as more of a caregiver and as soon as

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:04.500
we took that direction all of these different
threads started to grow out of it and before

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:13.069
long I had so much kind of backstory and possibility
for these characters that it was well beyond

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:19.510
the base concept of a 10 minute character
of thriller piece dealing with that moment

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.700
before the daughter’s death.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:28.060
And we kind of took off based on the people
who were getting involved and everything and

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.400
we realized that there was so much more to
the story.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.360
Not just bulking up that bit but that it was
just like a chunk of a story.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:45.340
So kind of like went and restructured that
to bring a little more depth to the characters

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:51.840
that were involved and these ideas of where
it could go, what it could all possibly mean.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:03:00.510
As the short kind of came into focus it became
apparent that it was more psychological than

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.250
horror and it was more family drama.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:08.950
And with that being the case there was much
more to tell than just that little bit.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:13.349
I had been involved on crowdfunding projects
before.

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:15.050
More in like a support kind of role.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.349
This is the first time that I’ve been the
person responsible for it and in charge of

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:20.349
it.

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:24.849
But in both of those instances we had something
to show.

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:28.989
One that I did with a collaborator John-Patrick
Driscoll in 2012.

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:33.739
We’d shot the short and were seeking funds
to complete it.

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:38.069
And one that I helped out with Tom Willimas
and Chris Lee.

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:45.970
We actually shot a very professional and very
structured concept trailer using who was going

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.510
to be our talent and everything.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:54.541
And from that experience I learned that you
want to have something to show (if at all

00:03:54.541 --> 00:03:55.541
possible).

00:03:55.541 --> 00:03:57.430
You don’t just want to get up there and
be like “Hi!

00:03:57.430 --> 00:03:58.430
I’m so-and-so.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:00.970
I have a great idea and you should give me
money.”

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.090
We wanted to actually show something.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:07.349
And also if we could see if it would potentially
work.

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:13.680
My initial plan was to let the film play the
festival circuit for about 6 months or so

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:21.170
to kind of build some buzz, some awareness
(things like that) and then go into the crowdfunding.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:25.710
But as it kept progressing, more and more
people found out about it.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.560
More people wanted to be involved with it.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:36.290
And basically we’d just kind of gotten to
place where it would work against us to wait

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.889
more than it would help.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:44.979
The way that it was structured is the last
scene of the short film is designed to work

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:52.130
in two ways (literally just two or three shots)
changed the entire scene.

00:04:52.130 --> 00:05:02.510
One version ends the short and it keeps it
exploring kind of a psychological illness

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.240
and disillusionment.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.910
And the other one springboards it to what
could be one or two years in the future.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:18.800
And it picks up again where the character
Clara (played by Ava Psoras is actually working

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.810
through the issues that she experiences in
the short.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:28.190
In that way it’s almost like a sequel to
the short but it’s told in one story and

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:31.509
the short in and of itself explores these
themes.

00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:32.870
It is a complete story.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:39.389
It has the beginning, middle and end but ends
on a more ambiguous, challenge you to think

00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:41.960
about reality kind of way.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:47.750
Whereas by tweaking the last scene, it gives
us a springboard to jump into the future and

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:51.729
basically see the effect that all of this
had on Clara.

00:05:51.729 --> 00:05:57.460
And then it gives us even more fun to play
around with her in a psychological sense because

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:04.080
then we get to do all kinds of things where
what’s real and what wasn’t real?

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.290
What from the short actually happened?

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:10.430
What from the short didn’t actually happen
and her entire reality gets to be blurred.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:16.320
And so basically the short is centered around
this whole idea of her dealing with guilt

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:23.949
over the sister, we take that and we just
like run wild with it in the feature.

00:06:23.949 --> 00:06:25.639
We begin to pick away her reality.

00:06:25.639 --> 00:06:29.500
We’re able to give her a life and take it
away from her.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:37.800
We’re able to give some kind of backstory,
some kind of a depth to her family because

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.490
at the root of it is really is about these
three girls.

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.300
And we’re really able to get into those
notions of family.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:50.470
And it’s really cool because we’re concentrating
on these female characters specifically and

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:56.930
we’re looking for kind of mother and daughter
relationships (sister relationships) and we’re

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:04.240
adding this kind of stress, this kind of family
dynamic which is dramatic by nature and we’re

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:10.290
putting it in a horror blanket except we’re
kind of playing with it psychologically.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:18.530
So basically we set Clara up in the short
and we knock her all over the place in the

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:19.590
feature.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:23.590
And we take all of these ideas and we run
with them further and we kind of complete

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:25.889
that story.

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:28.740
We are using the same footage, we are using
the same actors.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.560
I mean that was the plan once we start rolling
on the short.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:36.180
That this would be one quarter of the film.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.090
We’re independent, we’re very independent.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.740
We’re ultra-low budget.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:46.139
Everyone who is working on this is because
it’s a passion project of some sort.

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:55.389
We all believe in what we’re doing and ultimately
we don’t have the luxury or the time to

00:07:55.389 --> 00:07:58.210
reshoot anything actually.

00:07:58.210 --> 00:08:05.389
We were able to basically restructure the
opening act from the short film so Ava is

00:08:05.389 --> 00:08:06.460
still Clara.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:09.040
Betsy Lynn George is still Emily.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:11.819
Alexa Mechling is still Cassandra.

00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:15.690
Some other actors who appeared in the short
are also coming back for the feature including

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:20.819
Joe Fischell as Victor (Emily’s husband
and the girl’s father).

00:08:20.819 --> 00:08:25.790
Lynda Marnoni who worked with George Romero
in the 70’s in THE CRAZIES and SEASON OF

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:29.110
THE WITCH is coming back as Anastelle as well
as some others.

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:30.720
So yes, all of the same actors.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:32.190
All of the same footage.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:38.369
It’s quite literally two or three shots
that transition the short.

00:08:38.369 --> 00:08:44.660
The short is as stands the opening to the
feature with those few changes.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:51.079
So as I said before my initial idea was to
wait 6 months to a year before going on with

00:08:51.079 --> 00:08:52.699
the feature.

00:08:52.699 --> 00:08:57.170
But we had so many amazing opportunities kind
of present themselves such as Mia Zanotti

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:03.410
and other cast members and other opportunities
and places to shoot that it would actually

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:04.800
hurt us to wait.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:12.139
So the short film is being used as our ability
to show we’re more than just an idea.

00:09:12.139 --> 00:09:16.259
We’re doing this, we’ve done this, we’ve
started this.

00:09:16.259 --> 00:09:22.619
And that’s probably fro my personal experience
in crowdfunding the biggest piece of advice

00:09:22.619 --> 00:09:27.740
any filmmaker who is looking to do it on any
level.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:34.050
But be more than just a talking head saying
we’ve got this great idea.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:39.639
Have something to show even if you have to
rack up the credit card a little bit to put

00:09:39.639 --> 00:09:45.449
together some kind of a concept trailer with
the talent you hope to use, have something

00:09:45.449 --> 00:09:46.449
to show.

00:09:46.449 --> 00:09:53.879
So ultimately the way it ended up playing
out where we are, the short film for us is

00:09:53.879 --> 00:09:54.879
something to show.

00:09:54.879 --> 00:09:59.370
It’s our start, it’s our ability to say
we’re doing this.

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:03.500
It’s not just an idea, it’s something
that actually is happening.

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:08.949
And people can get a sense of what we’re
about and what we’re doing based off of

00:10:08.949 --> 00:10:14.400
that and then decide for themselves if they
think we’re worth supporting or not, which

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:15.649
of course, I hope they do.

